llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232): with op
offload, a MoE layer's expert weights in host memory are copied to the GPU
while the GPU waits. Not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
When most experts stay in system RAM (the usual way to run Qwen3.6 35B-A3B
or Qwen3-Coder-Next on an 8 GB card), a prompt batch of 32 tokens or more
runs each MoE layer on the GPU, and the scheduler first copies that layer's
experts there. The copy waits for the layer's router output, so it can copy
only the experts the batch uses, and it runs on the compute stream: the GPU
idles for the whole PCIe transfer of every MoE layer. With Qwen3.6 35B-A3B
Q4_K_M and all experts in host memory, skipping the copies (wrong output,
timing only) took a 2048-token prompt from 295 to 376 tok/s with 2048-token
batches, and from 167 to 388 with 512-token batches.

For a batch large enough that almost every expert is used (tokens x
experts per token >= 4 x experts, so under 2% go unused), copying all of
them costs the same and needs no router output, so it can start one layer
early. Such splits now get their host weights in one of two device slots
outside the graph allocator, alternating from one MoE split to the next. A
second backend instance on the same device, with its own stream, copies
the next split's weights into the other slot while the current split
computes; events order each copy after the slot's previous user and each
compute after its copy. Smaller batches keep the router-filtered copy.

The overlap needs the copy to run on a DMA engine. ROCm's runtime on gfx803
copies host to device with blit kernels unless HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1 (a 512 MiB
copy next to a GEMM loop: 451 ms with blit kernels against 452 ms serial,
407 ms with SDMA), so Dockerfile-Ollama sets HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1. With blit
kernels the prefetch is slower than no prefetch (512-token batches 170
against 193 tok/s).

VRAM: two slots, each the largest one split's host weights (one MoE layer's
experts), in place of the one copy the graph allocator would otherwise
place. ggml_backend_sched_get_buffer_size includes the slots, so memory
fitting counts them. GGML_SCHED_EXPERT_PREFETCH=0 disables the prefetch.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590, llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B heretic
Q4_K_M, -ncmoe 99, pp2048, tok/s):

| ubatch | before | SDMA only | SDMA + prefetch |
| ------ | ------ | --------- | --------------- |
| 512    | 193    | 213       | 243             |
| 1024   | 250    | 268       | 321             |
| 2048   | 295    | 304       | 367             |

Generation unchanged (tg32 about 23.5). llama-perplexity (512-token
chunks, 16 chunks, all experts in host memory) with prefetch and SDMA,
SDMA alone, and prefetch alone against neither: PPL 12.1753 in all four,
mean KLD 0.000000, same top token 100%. SDMA copy integrity: 3038 random
host-device round trips (1 B to 256 MiB, 767 GiB) with GEMMs running on
another stream, 0 mismatches, 0 GPU faults.

